              "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-Mqs0SRcpIvqnM2uVmxCqmxaeKvtXz0n/view",
              "totalPages": 27,
              "rawText": "FILE: Psychiatry-Anxiety.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 3 Psychiatry\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-Mqs0SRcpIvqnM2uVmxCqmxaeKvtXz0n/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 27\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\nAnxiety\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nAnxiety and related disease\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n2\n* **\n* *\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nAnxiety and related disease\n■ Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD)***\n■ Panic disorder***\n■ Agoraphobia\n■ Specific phobia\n■ Social anxiety disorder\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n3\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nGENERALIZED \nANXIETY DISORDER \n(GAD)\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nManagement of GAD\n■ Nonpharmacologic treatment\n– Psychoeducation\n– CBT\n■ Pharmacologic treatment\n– Antidepressants \n– Benzodiazepines\n– Others \nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n5\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nGoal of treatment\n■ To acutely reduce the severity and duration of \nthe anxiety/panic symptoms and to improve \noverall functioning\n■ Achieve and sustain remission with minimal or \nno anxiety symptoms and no functional \nimpairment\nDuration of therapy 12 months for most patients\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n6\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nTreatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n7\nRef: https://doi.org/10.1016/S0924-977X(99)00052-8\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nTreatment\n■ Acute treatment\n– Benzodiazepines\n– Antidepressants\n■ Long term treatment\n– Antidepressants\n– Benzodiazepine prn\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n8\nAdjunctive treatment\n▪ Beta blockers\n▪ Pregabalin\n▪ Quetiapine\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nTreatment\n9\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nTreatment\n10\nThe World Journal of Biological Psychiatry, 2008; 9(4): 248-312\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nTreatment\n11\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\nTreatment\n12\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nTreatment\n13\nJAMA.2022;328(24):2431-45.\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nTreatment: SSRIs\n■ First‐line drugs \n■ Withdrawal syndrome\n■ Adverse effects include headache, fatigue, \ndizziness, nausea, anorexia\n■ Weight gain and sexual dysfunction are \nlong‐term concerns\n■ Anxiolytic effect is delayed 2 ‐4 weeks (6 ‐8 \nweeks in PTSD, OCD)\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n14\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nTreatment: SNRIs\n■ Early adverse effects such as nausea, \nrestlessness, insomnia and headache may \nlimit compliance\n■ Sexual dysfunction long‐term\n■ Anxiolytics effects have latency of 2‐4 weeks\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n15\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nTreatment: TCAs\n■ Efficacy in all anxiety disorders is well ‐proven, \nexcept in SAD\n■ Avoid in elderly, patients with cardiovascular \ndisease, seizure disorders, and suicidal \nthoughts\n■ Dosage should be titrated up slowly; onset of \neffect is 2‐6 weeks, longer in OCD\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n16\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nTreatment: BZDs\n■ Safe and effective for short ‐term use; \nmaintenance requires evaluation of risks vs. \nbenefits\n■ Avoid in patients with history of substance or \nalcohol abuse\n■ Most commonly used in combination with \nSSRI/SNRI during first few weeks of therapy\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n17\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nTreatments: BZDs\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n18\nRef: Dipiro JT, et al. Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach 9th ,2014\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\nIndication of Antidepressant\n■ Depression\n■ Anxiety\n■ Neuropathic pain\n■ Dysthymia\n■ Bipolar with depressive type\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n19\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\nTreatments\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n20\nTreatment algorithm for \ngeneralized anxiety disorder.\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nPANIC DISORDER\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nManagement of PD\n■ Nonpharmacologic treatment\n– Avoid trigger substances\n■ Pharmacologic treatment\n– Antidepressants \n– Benzodiazepines\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n22\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nTreatment\n23\n\n--- [Page 24] ---\nTreatment\n24\n\n--- [Page 25] ---\nTreatment\n25\nJAMA.2022;328(24):2431-45.\n\n--- [Page 26] ---\nManagement of PD\n■ Acute phase: 1-3 months\n– Remission in 12 weeks for antidepressants\n– Alter drugs if no response in 6-8 weeks\n■ Maintenance\n– About 12-24 months – to prevent relapse\n– If little improvement, may need more than \n2 years\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n26\n\n--- [Page 27] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n27"
