            {
              "id": "1USJ4-2oPNa5G3XMup9INiTnJdgI139cg",
              "name": "Oncology-Lung cancer.pdf",
              "folder": "Chapter 11 Oncology",
              "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1USJ4-2oPNa5G3XMup9INiTnJdgI139cg/view",
              "totalPages": 30,
              "rawText": "FILE: Oncology-Lung cancer.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 11 Oncology\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1USJ4-2oPNa5G3XMup9INiTnJdgI139cg/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 30\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\nLUNG CANCER\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nIntroduction\n2\n■ Lung cancer, also known as lung carcinoma, is \na malignant lung tumor characterized by \nuncontrolled cell growth in tissues of the lung.\n– Small cell lung cancer (SCLC)\n– Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nPathogenesis\n3\n■ Molecular abnormalities in the pathogenesis of \nlung cancer\n– Aberrant promoter methylation\n– Activation of proto‐oncogenes, growth factors and \nreceptors\n– Expression of telomerase activity\n– Functional loss of tumor suppressor genes (many \nlead to increased cyclin‐dependent kinase activity \nand uncontrolled proliferation)\n– Alteration of apoptosis pathways favoring \nanti‐apoptosis\n– Potential loss of DNA repair mechanisms\nSalgia R, et al. J Clin Oncol 1998;16:1207‐1217., \nRosell R, et al. Lung Cancer 2004;46:135‐148\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nPathogenesis\n4\n■ K‐ras and EGFR (HER‐1, erb‐B1) \n– K‐ras mutations\n■ EML4‐ALK\nAhrendt SA, et al. Cancer 2001;92:1525‐1560.\nEberhard DA, et al.J Clin Oncol 2005;23:5905‐5909.\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nPathogenesis\n5\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nRisk factors\n6\n■ Tobacco (Passive smoking 1.5 fold increase in \nrisk, 3000 deaths per year)\n■ Radon, ionizing radiation ( 2nd  leading cause \nof lung cancer)\n■ Asbestos: 6X increased risk\n■ Occupational/environmental exposure to: \npetroleum, nickel, arsenic, chromates, \nhaloethers, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, \nvinyl chloride\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nRisk factors\n7\n■ Diet: Low levels of beta carotene and vitamin E \nlinked to lung cancer.\n■ Coexisting lung disease\n■ Genetic predisposition (Gene/environment \ninteraction most likely)\n– Family history 2x\n– Metabolism of carcinogens (CYP 2D6 genotype, \nglutathione S‐transferase M1 mutations)\n– Repair of DNA adducts (Nucleotide excision repair)\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nPathophysiology\n8\nSmall cell lung cancer (13‐20% of all lung cancer)\n■ General points\n– Most aggressive clinical course of all lung tumors \nif left untreated\n– Clear relationship to smoking\n– Initially highly sensitive to radiation and \nchemotherapy\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nPathophysiology\n9\nNon‐small cell lung cancer ( 80‐87% of all lung \ncancer)\n■ General points\n– Slower growing\n– Moderately sensitive to radiation\n– Low sensitivity to conventional chemotherapy\n■ Types\n– Adenocarcinoma (37‐47%)\n– Squamous cell (epidermoid) carcinoma (25‐32%)\n– Large cell carcinoma (10‐18%)\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nClinical presentation\n10\nแนวทางการตรวจวินิจฉัยและรักษาโรคมะเร็งปอด 2558\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nDiagnosis\n11\n■ Physical examination\n■ Laboratory testing\n– Serum electrolyte\n– Serum albumin\n– Liver function\n■ Chest X-ray\n■ PET imaging (PET scan)\n■ Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)\nPositron emission tomography (PET) scan\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\n14\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\n15\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nTreatment: SCLC\n16\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nTreatment: SCLC\n17\nLimited Stage\n■ Treatment: Concurrent chemoradiotherapy\n– Cisplatin‐etoposide with concurrent radiation has \nthe best supporting clinical data.\n– 4‐6 cycles of chemotherapy are given. \nMaintenance therapy is of no value\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nTreatment: SCLC\n18\nExtensive Stage\n■ Treatment: Chemotherapy\n– Cisplatin/carboplatin + etoposide / irinotecan x 4-\n6 cycle\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\n19\nแนวทางการตรวจวินิจฉัยและรักษาโรคมะเร็งปอด 2558\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\n20\nแนวทางการตรวจวินิจฉัยและรักษาโรคมะเร็งปอด 2558\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nTreatment: NSCLC\n21\nResectable (Early stage; stage I, II, IIIA)\n■ Surgery is common\n■ Consider adjuvant chemotherapy\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nAdjuvant chemotherapy for NSCLC\n22\nแนวทางการตรวจวินิจฉัยและรักษาโรคมะเร็งปอด 2558\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nTreatment: NSCLC\n23\nUnresectable (Stage IIIB, IV)\n■ Stage IIIB Platinum‐based chemotherapy + RT\n– Preferred regimens:\n■ Cisplatin 50 mg/m2 IV D1,8,29,36\n■ Etoposide 50 mg/m2 IV D1‐5, 29‐33\n■ RT – total 61 Gy\n– Alternative regimens:\n■ Cisplatin 100 mg/m2 IV D 1,29\n■ Vinblastine 5 mg/m2 weekly x 5\n■ RT – 63 Gy\n■ Stage IV: recommended palliative care\n\n--- [Page 24] ---\n24\nAdjuvant chemotherapy for NSCLC\nแนวทางการตรวจวินิจฉัยและรักษาโรคมะเร็งปอด 2558\n\n--- [Page 25] ---\nTreatment: NSCLC\n25\nUnresectable (Stage IV)\n■ EGFR mutation positive\n– Erlotinib\n■ EML4‐ALK Rearrangement positive\n– Crizotinib\n■ EGFR mutation negative\n– Bevacizumab, Cetuximab\n\n--- [Page 26] ---\nErlotinib\n26\n■ Erlotinib is mainly metabolized by the liver \nenzyme CYP3A4\n■ Common side effects include rash, diarrhea, \nmuscle pain, joint pain, and cough\n■ Serious side effects may include lung \nproblems, kidney problems, liver failure, \ngastrointestinal perforation, stroke, and \ncorneal ulceration\n\n--- [Page 27] ---\nErlotinib\n27\nErlotinib specifically targets the \nepidermal growth factor receptor \n(EGFR) tyrosine kinase, which is \nhighly expressed and occasionally \nmutated in various forms of \ncancer\n\n--- [Page 28] ---\nCrizotinib\n28\n■ Is an anti -cancer drug acting as an ALK \n(anaplastic lymphoma kinase) and ROS 1 (c-ros\noncogene 1) inhibitor\n\n--- [Page 29] ---\nComplication of lung cancer\n29\n■ SVC syndrome\n■ Pleural/pericardial effusion\n\n--- [Page 30] ---\n30"
            }
