              "rawText": "FILE: Dermatology Wound dressing.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 15 Dermatology\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1C8vuUy_m2_vid_VdtT0ngU-_qwkk6bva/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 19\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nWOUND CARE AND \nDRESSING\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nWound healing process\n5\nWound Healing Center at Inova Loudoun treats complex wound and ostomy cases; [cite 16 Aug 2025]. Available form: \nhttps://www.inovanewsroom.org/ilh/2017/05/wound-healing-center-at-inova-loudoun-treats-complex-wound-and-ostomy-cases/.\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nWound bed tissue\nBlack Necrotic tissue\nYellow Slough tissue\nRed Granulation tissue\nPink Epithelial tissue\n6\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nWound bed tissue\n7\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nPressure ulcer\n8\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nWound bed preparation\n9\nWorld Wide Wound. Teaching wound management: a collaborative model for future education; [cite 16 Aug 2025]. Available form: \nhttp://www.worldwidewounds.com/2005/november/Watret/Teaching-Wound-Mgt-Collaborative-Model.html\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nWound bed preparation\n■ TIME(S) model\n10\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nWound bed preparation\n■ NICE model\n– N Necrotic tissue\n– I Infection\n– C Characteristics\n– E Exudate\n11\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nWound solution\n■ 0.9% Sodium chloride [Normal saline]\n■ Alcohol 70%\n■ Tincture iodine\n■ Povidone iodine\n■ Chlrohexidine\n■ Hydrogen peroxide\n■ 0.5%Sodium hypochlorite [Dakin’s solution]\n12\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nWound dressing\n■ Gauze\n■ Tulles\n■ Film\n■ Foam\n■ Hydrogel\n■ Hydrocolloid\n■ Alginates\n■ Antimicrobial dressings\n13\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nWound dressing\nGauze\n➢ Woven cotton fibers\n➢ Permeable with desiccation; debridement; painful \nremoval\n14\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\nWound dressing\nTulles\n➢ Open-weave cloth soaked in soft paraffin or textiles \nchlorhexidine, or multilayered or perforated plastic \nfilms\n➢ Low adherent, suitable for flat, shallow wounds \nwith low exudates\n15\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nWound dressing\nFilm\n➢ Plastic (polyurethane); semipermeable\n➢ Allows water vapor permeation; adhesive; \nimpermeable for liquid sand bacteria\n16\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nWound dressing\nFoam\n➢ Hydrophilic (wound side) and hydrophobic (outer \nside) polyurethane or silicone foams; \nsemipermeable\n➢ Highly absorbent; for necrotic and exudative \nwounds\n17\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nWound dressing\nHydrogel\n➢ Water (96%) and polymer(polyethylene oxide)\n➢ Aqueous environment; requires secondary \ndressing; no adherence; not recommended if \ninfection is present; semipermeable\n18\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nWound dressing\nHydrocolloid\n➢ Hydrophilic colloidal particles and adhesive\n➢ Absorbs fluid; necrotic tissue autolysis; little \nadherence; occlusive\n19\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nWound dressing\nAlginates\n➢ Calcium and sodium salts of alginic acid found in \nbrown seaweed\n➢ Absorbs exudate and forms a hydrophilic gel after \nion exchange with wound fluid; not suited for dry \nwounds\n20\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nWound dressing\nAntimicrobial dressings\n➢ Silver-impregnated dressings\n➢ Iodine base \n➢ Metronidazole gel\n21"
