          "id": "17D5aoNJmiqNK_sLCyJ071BFc-ldGZLoP",
          "name": "Psychiatry-Bipolar disorder.pdf",
          "folder": "Chapter 3 Psychiatry",
          "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/17D5aoNJmiqNK_sLCyJ071BFc-ldGZLoP/view",
          "totalPages": 29,
          "rawText": "FILE: Psychiatry-Bipolar disorder.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 3 Psychiatry\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/17D5aoNJmiqNK_sLCyJ071BFc-ldGZLoP/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 29\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\nBIPOLAR DISORDER\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nClinical presentation\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n2\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nClinical presentation\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n3\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nNeurotransmitter alteration\n■ Major depressive disorders\n– 5-HT, DA, NE ↓\n■ Bipolar disorders\n– Depression: 5-HT, DA, NE ↓\n– Mania: 5-HT↓, DA and NE ↑\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n4\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nClassification of Bipolar\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n5\nMania +/- Depression\nDepression + Hypomania\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nLife cycle of Bipolar \nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n6\nRef: Grande I. Lancet  2016; 387: 1561-72\nPredominantly mania\nPredominantly depression\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nTreatment goal\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n7\n■ Response\n■ Remission\n■ Recovery\nMaintain function\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nStage of treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n8\nRef: Malhi GS, et al. Aust N Z J Psychiatry 2015;49:1087‐26.\n8 weeks\nSymptomatic\n1 year\nFunctional\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\n■ Lithium\n– acute (both mania and depression) and \nmaintenance therapy\n■ Antipsychotics (conventional and atypical)\n■ Antiepileptic drugs \n– VPA and CBZ in bipolar mania\n– LTG in bipolar depression\n■ Antidepressants: avoid in manic episode\n– high rate of switching: TCAs, SNRIs\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n9\nBenzodiazepine ?\nMania mood stabilizers\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n10\nRef: Malhi GS, et al. Drugs 2009;69:2063‐101.\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n11\n■ Acute management of bipolar mania: agitation\nCANMAT guideline 2018\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n12\n■ Acute management of bipolar I mania: mania\nCANMAT guideline 2018\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n13\n■ Acute management of bipolar I mania: mania\nCANMAT guideline 2018\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n14\n■ Acute management of bipolar I depression\nCANMAT guideline 2018\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n15\n■ Maintenance management of bipolar I depression\nCANMAT guideline 2018\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n16\n■ Acute management of bipolar II depression\nCANMAT guideline 2018\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nPharmacologic treatment*\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n17\n■ Maintenance management of bipolar II depression\nCANMAT guideline 2018\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nLithium\n■ Rapid and complete absorption after oral \nadministration: F 80-100%\n■ 95% drug excretion by the kidneys\n■ Long term use reduces suicide risk and \nmortality\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n18\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\nLithium\n■ Manic episode\n– Approved for mania and maintenance therapy\n– Onset of action: 1-2 weeks\n■ Depressive episode\n– Full effect: 4-6 weeks\n■ Narrow therapeutic index\n– Acute mania 0.8-1.5 mEq/L\n– Maintenance 0.6-1.2 mEq/L\n■ Monitor plasma level of Li at 12 hr after last \ndose\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n19\nPre lithium work up\nTFT (FT4, TSH)\nRenal function test\nBlood electrolyte\nUrinalysis\nMedication history\nHCG (pregnancy)\nCBC\nEKG\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\nLithium\nADR\n■ Fatigue, muscle weakness, obesity\n■ GI side effect (N/V/D) : 2-3 first weeks\n■ CNS : fine tremor (15-53%)\n■ Nephrotoxicity \n– polyuria, polydipsia, and nocturia (up to 70%)\n– hypercalcemia\n– impaired renal function ?\n■ Muscle weakness and lethargy : 30%\n■ Hypothyroidism\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n20\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nLithium\nLithium toxicity\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n21\nTOXIC\nEFFECT\nPLASMA  LEVEL \n(mmol/liter)\nSYMPTOMS AND SIGNS\nMild 1.0-1.5 Impaired concentration, lethargy,\nirritability, muscle weakness, tremor, \nslurred speech, and nausea\nModerate 1.6-2.5 Disorientation, confusion, drowsiness, \nrestlessness, unsteady gait, coarse \ntremor, dysarthria, muscle fasciculations, \nand vomiting\nSevere >2.5 Impaired conciousness (with progression\nto coma), delirium, ataxia, generalized \nfasciculations, extrapyramidal symptoms, \nconvulsions, and impaired renal function\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nLithium\nDrug interaction\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n22\n■ Increase lithium level\n– Thiazide diuretics\n– ACEIs, ARBs\n– NSAIDs (except ASA \nand sulindac)\n– Dehydration\n– Sodium loss\n– Elderly\n■ Decrease lithium level\n– Caffeine\n– Theophylline\n– High sodium intake\n– Acetazolamide\n– Osmotic diuretics\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nLamotrigine\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n23\nLTG-glucuronide\nLTG-arene oxide intermediate\nValproate\nRef: Bowden CL. Epilepsia 2002; 43: 53-9\n\n--- [Page 24] ---\nLamotrigine\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n24\nRef: Bowden CL. Epilepsia 2002; 43: 53-9\n\n--- [Page 25] ---\nQuetiapine\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n25\nRef: Jensen NH. Neuropsychopharmacology 2008; 33: 2303-12\n\n--- [Page 26] ---\nQuetiapine\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n26\n\n--- [Page 27] ---\nQuetiapine\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n27\n\n--- [Page 28] ---\nOther drugs ?\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n28\n■ Valproate\n■ Lurasidone\n■ Carbamazepine\n\n--- [Page 29] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n29"
