          "totalPages": 35,
          "rawText": "FILE: Psychiatry-Insomnia.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 3 Psychiatry\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1efNNZjGzackO4TVWpBP_PTEEPbLiypCI/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 35\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\nINSOMNIA\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nSleep cycle\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n2\nSlow wave sleep \nSleep latency\nGood sleep quality >>> NREM N3 + REM sleep\nNREM N1\nNREM N2\nNREM N3\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\n3\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nSleep-wake regulation\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n4\nErum JV. Neurosci Biobehav Rev. 2019;105:72-80.\nPedunculopontine nucleus (PPT) and Laterodorsal tegmental nucleus (LDT) \nTuberomammillary nucleus (TMN), Locus coeruleus (LC) and raphe nuclei (RN) Ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO), Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nNeurotransmitter\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n5\nErum JV. Neurosci Biobehav Rev. 2019;105:72-80.\nWakefulness\nSleep\nNREM REM\nMonoamine\n• Dopamine\n• Norepinephrine\n• Serotonin\nGABA Acetylcholine\nAcetylcholine Melatonin Serotonin ↓\nHistamine Norepinephrine ↓\nOrexin/hypocretin\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nDefinition\n■ Insomnia as a complaint of difficulty initiating \nor maintaining sleep or waking up too early or \npoor sleep quality\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n6\n➢ Initial insomnia (Difficulty falling asleep, DFA)\nDifficulty initiating asleep\n➢ Middle insomnia (Difficulty maintaining sleep, DMS)\n➢ Terminal insomnia (Early morning awakening, EMA)\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nDiagnosis\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n7\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\n3P model\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n8\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\n3P model\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n9\nRepresentation of The 3P Model of Insomnia, adapted from Spielman et al., (1987).\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nGoal of treatment\n■ Reduce symptoms and suffers from insomnia \nwith preserve quality of sleep\n■ Reduce morbidity\n■ Improve the quality of life for the patient and \nfamily\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n10\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nNon-pharmacotherapy\n■ Sleep restriction 2A\n■ Stimulus control 2A\n■ Sleep hygiene 2A\n■ Cognitive therapy 3A\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n11\nInsomnia Guideline in Thailand for adults 2025\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\nSleep hygiene\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n13\nRemove cause of insomnia: disease, medication …\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n14\nRemove cause of insomnia: disease, medication …\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n15\n■ Benzodiazepine\n■ Non-benzodiazepine (Z‐drugs)\n■ Melatonin and analogs\n■ Orexin receptor antagonist\n■ Sedating\n– antidepressants\n– antipsychotics\n– antihistamine\nThe ideal Hypnotic drugs\n• Rapid sleep induction\n• within 20 min\n• Sufficient duration\n• No “hangover”\n• Lacks abuse potential/tolerance\n• Mimics normal sleep pattern\n• High therapeutic index\n• No adverse effects or drug interactions\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n16\nBenzodiazepine\n■ All BZPs possess sedative and anxiolytic \nproperties\n■ Anxiolytic effect of BZPs are mediated by \nincrease GABA effect to activate GABA -A \nreceptor at the α2 subunit\n*\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n17\nBenzodiazepine\n■ Rapid onset and short duration\n– triazolam, midazolam\n■ Rapid onset and long duration\n– diazepam, flurazepam, clorazepate\n■ Intermediate onset and intermediate duration\n– lorazepam, alprazolam, temazepam\n■ Intermediate onset and long duration\n– clonazepam, chlordiazepoxide\nIndication of BZP ?\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n18\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n19\nBenzodiazepine\n■ Decrease sleep latency\n■ Decrease wake after sleep onset (WASO)\n■ Increase sleep efficiency\n■ Increase sleep quality\n■ Increase total sleep time\n■ Decrease REM sleep***\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n20\nBenzodiazepine\n■ Sedation/hangover reflect\n■ Anterograde amnesia\n■ Disinhibition (paradoxical effect)\n■ Cognitive impairment\n■ Complex sleep related behaviors (CSRB)\n■ Tolerance/physical dependence/addiction\n■ Withdrawal symptoms ---> Taper dose (10-25%)\nRespiratory depression ?\nAntidote ?\nFlumazenil\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n21\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n22\nNon-benzodiazepine (Z‐drugs): Zolpidem IR/ER\n■ Selective α1 subunit GABA receptor\n■ Rapid onset (30 min) short duration (6-8 hr)\n■ CYP3A4 substrate ---> inactive metabolite\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n23\nNon-benzodiazepine (Z‐drugs)\n■ Decrease sleep latency\n■ Decrease wake after sleep onset (WASO)\n■ Increase sleep efficiency\n■ Increase total sleep time\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n24\nNon-benzodiazepine (Z‐drugs)\n■ Hang over\n■ Amnesia\n■ Sedation\n■ Sleepwalk\n\n--- [Page 24] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n25\nMelatonin and analogs\nRef: Cardinali DP. J Pineal Res 2012 ;52(4):365‐75\n\n--- [Page 25] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n26\nMelatonin\n■ Dose: 0.3mg – 6mg nightly 30‐120 min before \nbedtime\n■ T1/2 = <60min; short‐acting; no hangover\nNot cognitive impairment\n\n--- [Page 26] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n27\nAgomelatine\n■ Mechanism: Melatonin agonist (MT1, MT2): \npromote sleep and antidepressant effect\n■ PK: metabolism mainly through CYP1A2 (90%)\n■ DI with CYP1A2 inhibitors; FQ, fluvoxamine\n■ ADR: headache, nausea and fatigue\nNot cognitive impairment\n\n--- [Page 27] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n28\nOrexin receptor antagonist (lemborexant)\n■ Dual orexin receptor antagonist (DORA)\n■ Indication for sleep onset and sleep \nmaintenance\n■ Decrease sleep latency\n■ Decrease wake after sleep onset (WASO)\n■ Increase sleep efficiency\n■ Increase total sleep time\n\n--- [Page 28] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n29\nOrexin receptor antagonist (lemborexant)\n\n--- [Page 29] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n30\nOrexin receptor antagonist (lemborexant)\n■ Not associated rebound insomnia and \nwithdrawal\n■ ADR\n– Somnolence\n– Headache\n– Nausea\n\n--- [Page 30] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n31\nSedating\nRef: Kennedy GJ. Prim Psychiatry 2007;14:29‐36.\n\n--- [Page 31] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n32\nInsomnia Guideline in Thailand for adults 2025\n\n--- [Page 32] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n33\nInsomnia Guideline in Thailand for adults 2025\n\n--- [Page 33] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n34\nInsomnia Guideline in Thailand for adults 2025\n\n--- [Page 34] ---\nPharmacologic treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n35\n\n--- [Page 35] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\n36"
