          "rawText": "FILE: Renal-Acid-Base disorder.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 5 Renal\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/109ml8hXjzzDiEGqfr_9e0-Phxsj984Kg/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 24\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nACID-BASE DISORDER\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nACID-BASE REGULATION\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n2\nRef: Tanis J and et-al .Acid-Base Balance Understanding is Critical to Treat Patients. Jan 15, 2016\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nACID-BASE REGULATION\n Chemical buffers\n bicarbonate/carbonic acid\n proteins\n phosphates   \n Respiratory regulation\n Renal regulation\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n3\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nBLOOD GAS VALUES\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n4\nArterial Blood Mixed Venous Blood \npH 7.40 (7.35–7.45) 7.38 (7.33–7.43)\nPaO 2 80–100 mm Hg 35–40 mm Hg \nSaO 2 95% 70%–75%\nPaCO 2 35–45 mm Hg 45–51 mm Hg\nHCO3 – 22–26 mEq/L (22–26 mmol/L) 24–28 mEq/L (24–28 mmol/L)\n   HCO3 –, bicarbonate; PaCO 2, partial pressure of carbon dioxide; \n   PaO 2, partial pressure of oxygen; SaO 2, saturation of arterial oxygen. \nRef : Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 8th edition\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nACID-BASE DISORDERS\npH Pco2 HCO3- Compensatory response\nMetabolic acidosis ↓ ↓ ↓ Immediate: respiratory compensation\nHyperventilation and decrease Pco2\nRespiratory acidosis ↓ ↑ ↑/\n Delayed: kidneys compensate by retaining HCO3-\nMetabolic alkalosis ↑ ↑ ↑ Immediate: respiratory compensation\nHypoventilation and increase Pco2\nRespiratory alkalosis ↑ ↓ ↓/\n Delayed: kidneys compensate through HCO3- loss\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n5\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n Loss HCO3-\n Endogenous acid ↑\n Acid excretion ↓\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n6\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (NAG MA) or\nHyperchloremic metabolic acidosis (HCMA)\n High anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAG MA) \nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n7\nAnion gap  = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3)\nNormal range of anion gap is 12  4 (8-16)\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (NAG MA) or\nHyperchloremic metabolic acidosis (HCMA)\n High anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAG MA) \nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n8\nMetabolic acidosis\nNormal anion gap\nBicarbonate \nloss Intake of \nchloride-\ncontaining \ncompounds\nHigh anion gap\nIncreased \nacid \nproduction\nIntake \norganic \nacid\nDecreased \nacid \nexcretion\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (NAG MA) or\nHyperchloremic metabolic acidosis (HCMA)\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n9\nDiarrhea Type 2 RTA\nCarbonic anhydrase inhibitors\nPotassium sparing diuretics\nChloride-containing compounds\n➢ HCl\n➢ NH4Cl\n➢ CaCl2\n➢ MgCl2\n➢ Arginine HCl\n➢ Cholestyramine\n***\nRTA: renal tubular acidosis\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (NAG MA) or\nHyperchloremic metabolic acidosis (HCMA)\n Management\n Alkali replacement \n Sodium bicarbonate\n Shohl’s solution (citric acid + sodium citrate)\n Sodium citrate\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n10\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n High anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAG MA) \nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n11\nKetoacid\nLactic acid\n▪ Type A\n▪ Type B\nFormic acid\nSalicylate\nEthanol\nEthylene glycol\nRenal failure\nAcetone \nAcetoacetic acid (AcAc) \nBeta-Hydroxybutyric acid (B- OH BA)\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n High anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAG MA) \n Management\n Removed precipitating factors\n Alkaline therapy [keep pH 7.2]\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n12\nHCO3 deficit = 0.5 x Wt (kg) x (HCO3 desired– HCO3 measured )\nNormal  range of HCO3 is 22-26 mEq/L (mmol/L)\n7.5% Sodium bicarbonate (8.92 mEq/10 ml)\n8.4% Sodium bicarbonate (10 mEq/10 ml)\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nMETABOLIC ACIDOSIS\n High anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAG MA) \n Alkali therapy\n ADR\n Hypernatremia\n Hypokalemia\n Hypercapnia\n Volume overload\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n14\nParadoxical transient intracellular acidosis\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nMETABOLIC ALKALOSIS\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n15\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nMETABOLIC ALKALOSIS\n Risk factor\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n16\nLoss of H+ Gain of HCO3\nStomach: Vomiting\n                  NG tube suction\nKidney: Hyperaldosteronism, \n              Bartter’s syndrome\nMilk-alkali syndrome\nExcessive use of alkali therapy\nClassification by 0.9% NaCl response\n▪ Saline responsive\n▪ Saline resistance\n อาการและอาการแสดง\n ผู ้ ป ่ วยมีภาวะขาดน ้า\n คลอไรด์ในปัสสาวะ < 15 mEq/L\n ปัสสาวะเป ็ นกรด (pH < 5.5)\n Hypokalemia (K+ < 3.5 mEq/L)\n สาเหตุ\n การอาเจียน\n การใช ้สายสวนกระเพาะอาหาร (NG tube)\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nMETABOLIC ALKALOSIS\n Management saline responsive\n Removed precipitating factors\n 0.9% NaCl replacement therapy\n KCl supplement (If K < 3.5 mEq/L)\n 0.1 N HCl (as need)\n Acetazolamide \n 250-500mg PO TID x 3-5 days\n 500mg IV single dose\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n17\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nMETABOLIC ALKALOSIS\n Management saline responsive\n 0.1 N HCl (as need)\n Infusion rate should not exceed 0.2 mEq/kg/hr\n D5W or NSS\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n18\nDose of HCl (mEq) = 0.5 x body weight (kg) x (plasma bicarbonate – 24)\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nRESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n19\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\nRESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS\n Risk factor\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n20\nCentral nervous system depression:\n- Drugs (anesthetics, opioids, sedative)\n- Central sleep apnea\n- Obesity hypoventilation syndrome\nAirway/lung parenchymal disease:\n- Upper airway obstruction\n- Asthma, COPD\n- Pulmonary edema\n- Drug (beta-blocker)\nNeuromuscular disease:\n- Guillain-Barre’ syndrome \n- Myasthenia gravis\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\nRESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS\n Management\n Correct of underlying cause\n Bronchodilator\n Mechanical ventilation\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n21\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nRESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n22\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nRESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS\n Risk factors\n Hypoxemia\n โรคปอด : pneumonia, pulmonary embolism\n มีการกระตุ้นระบบประสาทส่วนกลาง-การหายใจ เช่น \ncerebrovascular accident, ไข้, โรคตับ, ภาวะตั้งครรภ์, ผู้ที่อยู่\nในที่สูง salicylate intoxication\n Hyperventilation\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n23\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nRESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS\n Management\n Correct underlying cause\n Supportive treatment\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\n24"
